Title: "Linear Transformations"
Id: "165a0b80-16b8-4616-9d9d-95efe4751d2d"


Text: "A *linear transformation* (or linear map) $T : V \to W$ between vector spaces
       over the same field $F$ is a function that preserves vector addition and scalar
       multiplication:
       $$ T(u + v) = T(u) + T(v) \quad \text{and} \quad T(c \cdot v) = c \cdot T(v). $$
       Equivalently, $T$ preserves all linear combinations: $T(\sum c_i v_i) = \sum c_i T(v_i)$.
       Linear maps are the structure-preserving morphisms of linear algebra, forming
       the vector space $\mathcal{L}(V, W) = \operatorname{Hom}_F(V, W)$ under pointwise
       addition and scalar scaling."
Id: "4467454a-53b1-4a53-b40f-76086fdf6fbe"


SectionTitle: "Linear Maps"
Id: "bb7bb487-0b2a-4844-bafe-31c3d02c3f4d"


[\linear.map:from{V}:to{W}]
Declares: T(v__)
when:
. V is \set
. W is \set
specifies: T is \function:on{V}:to{W}
satisfies:
. forAll: u, v is \\anything
  then: T(u \.vector.+./ v) = T(u) \.vector.+./ T(v)
. forAll: c, v is \\anything
  then: T(c \.vector.*./ v) = c \.vector.*./ T(v)
Documented:
. called: "linear map from $V?$ to $W?$"
. description: "A linear transformation $T : V \to W$ is a map preserving vector
                addition and scalar multiplication."
Id: "e18d1a96-65a7-42f8-9f22-8458b4aab35f"


SectionTitle: "Kernel and Image"
Id: "a86d1afc-c089-469e-ac6c-dcbd2e42e696"


Text: "The *kernel* (or null space) $\ker(T) = \{v \in V : T(v) = 0\}$ consists
       of all vectors annihilated by $T$. The kernel is a subspace of $V$, and
       measures the failure of $T$ to be injective:
       $T(u) = T(v) \iff u - v \in \ker(T)$. Consequently, $T$ is injective if
       and only if $\ker(T) = \{0\}$. For any $w \in \operatorname{im}(T)$, the
       fiber $T^{-1}(w)$ is an affine subspace of the form $v_0 + \ker(T)$."
Id: "ab265e43-99d4-4a94-bbcc-11ccc4490b62"


[\kernel:of{T(v__)}]
Defines: K := \set@{v_ : v_ "in" V | T(v_) = 0}
using:
. V is \set
. W is \set
when: T is \function:on{V}:to{W}
Documented:
. called: "kernel of $T?$"
. written: "\ker(T?)"
. description: "The kernel $\ker(T) = \{v \in V \mid T(v) = 0_W\}$ is a subspace
                of $V$."
Id: "75e0201a-11d9-4c46-8b45-447b6610ca15"


Text: "The *image* (or range) $\operatorname{im}(T) = \{T(v) : v \in V\}$ is the
       subspace of $W$ consisting of all output vectors attained by $T$. The
       dimension of the image is called the *rank* of $T$,
       $\operatorname{rank}(T) = \dim(\operatorname{im} T)$, while the dimension
       of the kernel is the *nullity*, $\operatorname{null}(T) = \dim(\ker T)$."
Id: "844e6471-8710-46bd-acde-bffc5b914a93"


[\image:of{T(v__)}]
Defines: Im := \set@{T(v_) : v_ "in" V}
using:
. V is \set
. W is \set
when: T is \function:on{V}:to{W}
Documented:
. called: "image of $T?$"
. written: "\operatorname{im}(T?)"
. description: "The image $\operatorname{im}(T) = \{T(v) \mid v \in V\}$ is a
                subspace of $W$."
Id: "fa8a1bfb-bd31-4f60-8b55-02e1f4e878f7"


SectionTitle: "Isomorphisms and Rank-Nullity"
Id: "bf47d72c-46ac-41b4-874d-89923f888495"


[\(isomorphic)::vector.space:over{F}]
Refines: V
when: F is \field
specifies: V is \vector.space:over{F}
satisfies:
. exists:
  . W is \vector.space:over{F}
  . T(v__) is \linear.map:from{V}:to{W}
  suchThat: T is? \(bijective)::function:?on{V}:?to{W}
Documented:
. adjective: "isomorphic"
. description: "Two vector spaces are isomorphic if there exists a bijective
                linear transformation between them."
Id: "d1239479-6b9d-429a-b3e5-2e70a1529638"


Text: "The Fundamental Theorem of Linear Maps—the Rank-Nullity
       Theorem—establishes the conservation of dimension under linear mappings:
       the dimension of the domain is apportioned precisely between the
       directions compressed to zero (the nullity) and the directions preserved
       in the target space (the rank)."
Id: "0fd6e4c3-760e-41e1-a76d-86b9f5f12a39"


[\rank.nullity.theorem]
Theorem:
given:
. F is \field
. V is \vector.space:over{F}
. W is \vector.space:over{F}
. T(v__) is \linear.map:from{V}:to{W}
then:
. exists: d1, d2, d3 is \natural
  suchThat:
  . d1 = \dimension:of{V}
  . d1 = d2 \.natural.+./ d3
Documented:
. called: "Rank-Nullity Theorem"
. description: "For any linear transformation on a finite-dimensional space,
                $\dim(V) = \dim(\ker T) + \dim(\operatorname{im} T)$."
Proof: """
Let $V$ be finite-dimensional with $n = \dim(V)$, and let $T : V \to W$ be a linear transformation.
The kernel $\ker(T)$ is a subspace of $V$. Let $\{u_1, \dots, u_k\}$ be a basis for $\ker(T)$,
so the nullity is $\dim(\ker T) = k$.
By the Basis Extension Theorem, extend this to a basis $\{u_1, \dots, u_k, v_1, \dots, v_m\}$ of $V$,
so $n = k + m$.

We claim that $S = \{T(v_1), \dots, T(v_m)\}$ is a basis for the image $\operatorname{im}(T)$:
1. **Spanning**: Let $w \in \operatorname{im}(T)$, so $w = T(v)$ for some $v \in V$.
   Expanding $v$ in the basis of $V$:
   $$ v = \sum_{i=1}^k a_i u_i + \sum_{j=1}^m b_j v_j. $$
   Applying linearity and using $T(u_i) = 0$:
   $$ w = T(v) = \sum_{i=1}^k a_i T(u_i) + \sum_{j=1}^m b_j T(v_j) = 0 + \sum_{j=1}^m b_j T(v_j). $$
   Thus $S$ spans $\operatorname{im}(T)$.
2. **Linear Independence**: Suppose $\sum_{j=1}^m c_j T(v_j) = 0$.
   By linearity, $T(\sum_{j=1}^m c_j v_j) = 0$, so the vector $v^* = \sum_{j=1}^m c_j v_j \in \ker(T)$.
   Expanding $v^*$ in the basis $\{u_i\}$ of $\ker(T)$ gives $v^* = \sum_{i=1}^k d_i u_i$, so:
   $$ \sum_{i=1}^k (-d_i) u_i + \sum_{j=1}^m c_j v_j = 0. $$
   Since $\{u_1, \dots, u_k, v_1, \dots, v_m\}$ is linearly independent in $V$, all coefficients
   must vanish: in particular, $c_1 = \dots = c_m = 0$.

Hence $S$ is a basis for $\operatorname{im}(T)$, establishing $\operatorname{rank}(T) = \dim(\operatorname{im} T) = m$.
Therefore:
$$ \dim(V) = k + m = \dim(\ker T) + \dim(\operatorname{im} T). $$
"""
Id: "13696819-0077-4466-954d-be89bc6a62af"


TextTheorem: "The First Isomorphism Theorem for Vector Spaces. For any linear
              map $T : V \to W$, the induced mapping
              $\widetilde{T} : V / \ker(T) \to \operatorname{im}(T)$ defined by
              $\widetilde{T}(v + \ker T) = T(v)$ is a well-defined vector space
              isomorphism: $$ V / \ker(T) \cong \operatorname{im}(T). $$ The
              quotient removes precisely the directions collapsed by $T$,
              leaving a faithful copy of the image. Equating dimensions on both
              sides immediately recovers the Rank-Nullity Theorem:
              $\dim(V) - \dim(\ker T) = \dim(\operatorname{im} T)$."
Id: "a615de22-89bb-4bf1-aa10-3b1b919191a4"


Theorem:
given:
. F is \field
. V is \vector.space:over{F}
. W is \vector.space:over{F}
. T(v__) is \linear.map:from{V}:to{W}
where: \dimension:of{V} = \dimension:of{W}
then: T is? \(injective)::function:?on{V}:?to{W}
iff: T is? \(surjective)::function:?on{V}:?to{W}
Documented:
. called: "Injective--Surjective Equivalence in Equal Dimension"
. description: "For a linear map between finite-dimensional spaces of equal
                dimension, injectivity and surjectivity are equivalent."
Proof: """
Let $\dim(V) = \dim(W) = n < \infty$.
By the Rank-Nullity Theorem:
$$ \dim(V) = \dim(\ker T) + \dim(\operatorname{im} T) \implies n = \dim(\ker T) + \dim(\operatorname{im} T). $$

- $(\implies)$: Suppose $T$ is injective. Then $\ker(T) = \{0\}$, so $\dim(\ker T) = 0$.
  The formula yields $\dim(\operatorname{im} T) = n - 0 = n = \dim(W)$.
  Since $\operatorname{im}(T)$ is a subspace of $W$ possessing the same dimension as $W$,
  it must equal the entire codomain: $\operatorname{im}(T) = W$. Thus $T$ is surjective.

- $(\impliedby)$: Suppose $T$ is surjective. Then $\operatorname{im}(T) = W$, so $\dim(\operatorname{im} T) = \dim(W) = n$.
  The formula yields $\dim(\ker T) = n - n = 0$.
  The only subspace of dimension $0$ is the trivial subspace $\{0\}$, so $\ker(T) = \{0\}$.
  Thus $T$ is injective.

Hence for linear operators on equal finite-dimensional spaces, injectivity, surjectivity,
and invertibility (bijectivity) are all equivalent.
"""
Id: "9519b138-f788-4156-8fd1-7d01b955decf"


[\projection:on{V}]
Declares: P(v__) is \linear.map:from{V}:to{V}
using: F is \field
when: V is \vector.space:over{F}
satisfies:
. forAll: v "in" V
  then: P(P(v)) = P(v)
Documented:
. called: "projection on $V?$"
. description: "A projection is an idempotent linear endomorphism."
Id: "a9208468-06d8-4617-baef-6970565693b4"
